\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2}{\ignorespaces Condition-specific accuracy across network families. Each point is the endpoint-tuned cell RMSE divided by a comparator's cell RMSE; values below one favor endpoint tuning. Bars are 95\% seed-block bootstrap intervals on a logarithmic horizontal axis. The same seed draws generate all ratios. Adding spillover adjustment helps relative to assignment-only estimators in strong-linear and cluster conditions, but the linear exposure-aware comparator is usually better.}}{6}{figure.caption.7}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:accuracy}{{2}{6}{Condition-specific accuracy across network families. Each point is the endpoint-tuned cell RMSE divided by a comparator's cell RMSE; values below one favor endpoint tuning. Bars are 95\% seed-block bootstrap intervals on a logarithmic horizontal axis. The same seed draws generate all ratios. Adding spillover adjustment helps relative to assignment-only estimators in strong-linear and cluster conditions, but the linear exposure-aware comparator is usually better}{figure.caption.7}{}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3}{\ignorespaces Two limits of observed-data assessment. (a) Near-unit fitted residuals coexist with substantial absolute global-effect errors; all scientific records are shown, not independent graph samples. (b) For the same strong-linear outcome form, individual and cluster assignment yield different endpoint-tuned RMSEs across families. Bars are 95\% seed-block intervals. This design contrast changes the joint assignment distribution, not just one isolated exposure statistic.}}{7}{figure.caption.8}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:diagnostics}{{3}{7}{Two limits of observed-data assessment. (a) Near-unit fitted residuals coexist with substantial absolute global-effect errors; all scientific records are shown, not independent graph samples. (b) For the same strong-linear outcome form, individual and cluster assignment yield different endpoint-tuned RMSEs across families. Bars are 95\% seed-block intervals. This design contrast changes the joint assignment distribution, not just one isolated exposure statistic}{figure.caption.8}{}}
